    "result_text": "All 34 tests pass with a green build. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\n`membershipTier` now returns `'GOLD'` when the owner's household (owners sharing the same `householdId`) has 3+ members after the create; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply.\n\nFollowing the codebase's existing pattern (`namesakeCount` is computed at create time and stored on the `Owner`, then the mapper derives the tier from it), I did the same for household size:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `householdSize` field (`household_size` column) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 after household backfill and setting `householdId`, computes and stores `householdSize` = existing members carrying the shared `householdId` + 1 (the new owner). Added a `countHouseholdMembers(householdId)` helper that returns 0 for owners not in any household (null `householdId`), so those stay a household of one.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.membershipTier`** \u2014 returns `'GOLD'` when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise falls through to the unchanged SILVER/BRONZE logic.\n\n4. **Schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `household_size` column. Tests run on `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column had to exist in `schema.sql`.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` description to document the GOLD rule (field is a plain string, no enum to constrain).\n\nBecause the size is captured at each owner's own create time, the GOLD promotion reflects \"3+ members after this create\" per the spec \u2014 the third household member reads back as `GOLD` (verified by `Cp23Tests`), while the existing SILVER/BRONZE tests (`Cp15Tests`) and household-id tests (`Cp11Tests`) remain green.",
